{% tool-context
   title="Sharding Operations Lab"
   question="Can a shard plan stay balanced when one key gets hot, an owner fails, or data must move online?"
   definition="Sharding assigns each key to one authoritative storage owner. The placement rule controls locality and balance; replication protects availability but does not split a hot key or remove the cost of moving ownership."
   inputs="Choose hash, range, or directory placement; shape the key distribution and shard count; then change traffic, write share, replication, read routing, and the rebalance budget."
   outcome="Trace exact key ownership, read and write paths, per-shard load, busiest-owner headroom, availability, replica lag, movement cost, and the consequence users experience."
   experiment="Compare a hot key, tenant skew, shard loss, online reshard, replica lag, and recovery catch-up. Find a configuration that stays available without hiding stale reads or exhausting the busiest shard." /%}

